//! #7563, #7452: a fold step that writes an array slot by a negative index (`.[-$i] = v`, `.[-1] += 1`,
//! `.[-$i] |= f`) or by a padding one (`.[$t][799] = 1` from `[]`, `.[$t][$t] = 1`, `.a[30]` under a new
//! row) declined the in-place fold write, so every step went through the re-index bridge, which copied
//! the whole accumulator: the fold was quadratic where jq 1.7.1 is linear. The in-place write now takes
//! both, through the same `set_index` the evaluator writes with; an index still negative after counting
//! back from the end, and a negative index into `null`, stay the evaluator's, with jq's error.
//!
//! Every expected line was captured from jq 1.7.1 (`/usr/bin/jq`); jq 1.8.2 answers the same, but for
//! the reworded `Cannot index string with number` of a string target, a 1.8 improvement that keeps
//! 1.7.1 as the reference. The in-place proof is the unit test
//! `owned_assign_step_takes_negative_and_padding_indexes_in_place_7563` in `src/jq/eval.rs`.

use anyhow::Result;

use crate::run_jq_full;

